<title>Network.fetchBytes Action</title>


<subheader>Format</subheader>

<logicsyntax>
s = CS.Network.fetchBytes(<arg>url</arg> := ...,
                       『, <arg>method</arg> := ...』
                       『, <arg>headers</arg> := ...』
                       『, <arg>body</arg> := ...』『, <arg>bodyFormat</arg> := ...』
                       『, <arg>detailed</arg> := ...』
                       『, <arg>responseHeadersFormat</arg> := ...』
                       『, <arg>responseBodyFormat</arg> := ...』)
</logicsyntax>

<indent><table 3 header nowrap>
    <cell>Argument</cell><cell>Description</cell><cell nowrap>Types / Required</cell>
    <cell><arg>url</arg></cell><cell>The URL to fetch.</cell><cell>string~!~required</cell>
    <include fetch_arguments_optional.hgi />
    <cell><arg>responseBodyFormat</arg></cell><cell>The <link action_invoker_binary_data.csdoc>format</link> in which the bytes are returned.~!~The default value is <logiccolor>"dataUrl"</logiccolor>.</cell><cell>string~!~not required</cell>
</table></indent>


<subheader>Description</subheader>

The <action>Network.fetchBytes</action> <link action_invoker.csdoc>action</link> submits a HTTP request to a server and returns the response, with or without details, as bytes. The argument <arg>responseBodyFormat</arg> allows you to specify the <link action_invoker_binary_data.csdoc>format</link> of the returned binary data. If requesting a large amount of data, consider using the <logiccolor>"cache"</logiccolor> format to avoid representing a huge number of bytes as a string.

This action is a wrapper around the actions <action>Network.fetch</action> and <action>Network.fetchBody</action>. <include fetch_convenience_methods.hgi />


<include fetch_url.hgi />

<include fetch_method.hgi />

<include fetch_headers.hgi />

<include fetch_body.hgi />

<include fetch_detailed_response.hgi />


<subheader>Return Value</subheader>

By default, the action returns a string with the HTTP response body represented as a string in one of the <link action_invoker_binary_data.csdoc>output formats</link> used for binary data. If <arg>detailed</arg> is <logiccolor>true</logiccolor>, the action returns an object with the response body as the binary data string and details about the response as described above.


<subheader>Exceptions</subheader>

The action throws an exception if any of its arguments are not specified in a valid form, or if:

<list>
    <include fetch_exceptions.hgi />
</list>


<subheader>Example (CSPro Logic)</subheader>

<logic>
// retrieve an image from the CSPro open source repository, caching the content
// rather than receiving the bytes in the default form, as a data URL
string cacheKey = CS.Network.fetchBytes(
    url := "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/csprousers/cspro/refs/heads/dev/cspro/html/images/cspro-logo-medium.png",
    responseBodyFormat := "cache"
);

// use the cached content to update an Image object
Image logo;
logo.setValueFromJson(
    maketext('{ "content": { "url": %s } }', encode(JsonString, cacheKey))
);
</logic>


<seealso CS_Network.csdoc />
